-- Migration 280: restore the missing English passages to 64 stems, and strip the
-- page-footer bleed from 43 stems. 105 rows, 21 papers.
--
-- Two defects, one table. (1) Twelve English direction blocks carry a
-- reading passage, a numbered cloze passage or a sentence list. None
-- reached the import: the extractor counted context blocks but never stored
-- their text, so 64 stems ask about a passage that is not there. (2) A page
-- footer of the form "69 | P a g e" bled into 43 stems when the PDF text
-- layer was read across a page break.
--
-- Both are recovered from the PDF text layer, never invented. Every passage
-- below is the direction block verbatim, from its header to the first
-- question marker of its range, with page-footer lines dropped and
-- whitespace collapsed to single spaces -- the same normalisation every
-- loaded row already carries. No passage was genuinely absent from the
-- source: all twelve reconstruct in full, so no English row is left
-- needs_correction.
--
-- HOW THE ROWS WERE CHOSEN. The set, not the stem, is the unit. A review
-- pass that matches stems mentioning "the passage", "the author" or "blank
-- (a)-(f)" finds 28 English rows; that undercounts, because a sibling like
-- ENG-2020-Q022 ("Initially, the role of the World Bank was to:") never
-- names the passage and is just as unanswerable without it. Attaching each
-- block to every question in its printed range gives 64.
--
-- ONE SET BEYOND THE PASSAGES: ENG-2020 Q.48-49 asks which sentence comes
-- fifth and last "after rearrangement", and the six sentences to rearrange
-- live in the direction block, not the stems. Same defect, same fix; called
-- out here because it is not a reading passage and would not be found by
-- looking for one.
--
-- FOOTER STRIP, and how it was proved safe. Pattern: a run of 1-4 digits
-- preceded by whitespace or start-of-string, then "| P a g e". All 43
-- matches are anchored that way, each stem carries exactly one, and no
-- option row carries any. Each strip was checked by deleting the matched
-- span from the whitespace-normalised original and asserting the result
-- equals the repaired text character for character: 43 of 43 pass, and
-- every one removes exactly 13 characters. 24 footers sit at the end of a
-- stem, 19 in the middle -- the middles are the reason this matters,
-- because there the footer reads as content.
--
-- Two rows carry both defects (ENG-2022-Q025 and ENG-2023-Q024); their
-- single UPDATE strips the footer and then prepends the passage.
--
